sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/darwin1859-ch04-16> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Because all freshwater basins together form a small area, competition among freshwater organisms has been less severe, so new forms have arisen more slowly and old forms have been exterminated more slowly there, which is why fresh water holds ancient and anomalous forms.";
    dc:type "prediction";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Freshwater biotas evolve and go extinct more slowly, keeping relict forms";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Charles Darwin, On the Origin of Species (1st ed., London: John Murray, 1859), Chapter IV, “Natural Selection”; section “Circumstances favourable and unfavourable to Natural Selection, namely, intercrossing, isolation, number of individuals”. Scope: freshwater organisms, compared with marine and terrestrial ones"
}
